  "step_2": "The individual states that the 100 Euro difference between the non-zero outcomes (2500 vs. 2400) was not important to them ('berührten mich wenig'). Therefore, they focused on the probabilities. They note that the probabilities were 'almost equal' but chose the option with the 'higher' probability. Given that they refer to a higher probability after dismissing the outcome difference, and since Lottery B has a slightly higher probability of the non-zero outcome (34% vs. 33%), it is likely they are referring to the higher chance of winning something. However, the phrasing 'die höhere gewählt' (chose the higher one) in the context of probabilities suggests they preferred the option with the higher probability, though it is ambiguous which probability (of winning or losing) they mean. Their justification centers on comparing probabilities after deeming outcome differences negligible.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires focusing on the probability of the minimum outcome (i.e., 0 Euros) and choosing the lottery with the higher such probability (Lottery A, 67% > 66%). However, the individual appears to have focused on the probabilities of winning (non-zero outcomes), noting they are almost equal but choosing the one with the 'higher' probability. This suggests attention to the 34% vs. 33% chance of winning, not the 66% vs. 67% chance of losing. Therefore, the information considered by the individual (probability of winning) differs from that required by the decision reason (probability of losing). Additionally, the justification—choosing the option with the higher probability of winning—is inconsistent with the rule of the decision reason, which would favor the option with the higher probability of the minimum (losing) outcome. Hence, the individual did not use this decision reason. There is no indication they considered the probability of the worst outcome as a basis for preference.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
